WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.728
An algorithm is a precise sequence of steps for solving a class of problems

00:00:05.728 --> 00:00:14.100
To find ten in this sorted list, binary search compares with a middle value instead of inspecting every entry from the beginning.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:21.454
Comparing ten with the lower middle value eight eliminates every entry up to eight

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:27.508
Sorted order justifies the elimination: none of those smaller values can be the target

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:31.400
The remaining candidates are ten, twelve, fourteen, and sixteen.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:39.718
Comparing with twelve narrows the search again, leaving ten as the only candidate on its left

00:00:39.718 --> 00:00:45.858
Each step preserves the invariant that a present target remains inside the candidate range

00:00:45.858 --> 00:00:49.367
Shrinking that range ensures the procedure eventually terminates.

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:54.316
Repeated halving explains the logarithmic growth in comparisons

00:00:54.316 --> 00:01:00.246
This efficiency depends on ordered data and a method for accessing middle entries

00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:08.000
Without sorted order, discarding half could throw away the target, so speed cannot replace the algorithm's assumptions.
